<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as atomic bombing · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The atomic bombings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> were the attacks by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> on two Japanese cities in August 1945, the only uses of nuclear weapons in war. They killed tens of thousands of people immediately and many more afterwards. Together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-declaration-of-war-on-japan">Soviet declaration of war</a>, they led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki.jpg" alt="Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George R. Caron / Charles Levy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atomic_bombing_of_Japan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
